@startmindmap exp3_map
title Experiment 3 - map: fetch_add instead of a mutex (2 threads, one counter)
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF

* Exp 3: atomic fetch_add
** Question
*** Same loop, same CPUs, same counter: what changes when the lock becomes one atomic instruction?
** Setup
*** Identical to Experiment 2 except the counter type
*** One line of difference: FetchAddCounter replaces MutexCounter
** Result (mean of 5 runs)
*** 8.79 ns per increment, slowest thread
*** 228.3 M increments per second total
*** 0 percent kernel time
*** 0 voluntary context switches
*** About 2.8 times faster than the mutex
** Lesson
*** Atomicity does not need a lock object or a syscall
*** The line itself still moves between cores, so it is not free
** Concepts to read first
*** Concept 06: fetch_add vs CAS
*** Concept 07: the cache line moves between cores
** Compared with
*** Experiment 2: the mutex baseline
@endmindmap
